direction: down

title: |md
  ## mprog: ordered, multi-program attach
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

problem: "Classic tc problem" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  p_body: |md
    Multiple BPF programs attached to one hook?
    With classic tc, you used **filter priorities**:
    - tc filter add ... pref 100 ...
    - tc filter add ... pref 200 ...

    Reordering = delete + re-add.
    No atomic insert "before X" or "after Y".
    No clean way to coordinate between owners.
  |
}

mprog: "mprog (multi-program) abstraction" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  m_body: |md
    Kernel infra introduced with tcx, also used by netkit.
    Provides:
    - bpf_link-based ownership
    - flag-based ordering at attach time
    - inspection: list of currently-attached progs

    Flags:
    - BPF_F_BEFORE  ← prepend before existing
    - BPF_F_AFTER   ← append after existing
    - BPF_F_REPLACE ← replace specific link
    - BPF_F_LINK    ← attach via link FD
  |
}

example: "Example: chain three programs" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  e_body: |md
    Order: counter → fw → tracer

    P1 (counter)        attach BEFORE
    P2 (fw)             attach AFTER existing
    P3 (tracer)         attach BEFORE existing → puts P3 first

    Final order at runtime:
    P3 → P1 → P2

    Each program returns:
    - TC_ACT_OK         continue chain
    - TC_ACT_SHOT       drop, stop chain
    - TC_ACT_REDIRECT   redirect, stop chain
  |
}

problem -> mprog
mprog -> example
